Sphinx research summary focused on metadata/tagging:

Document-level metadata in Sphinx is done with field lists at top of .rst files.
Example:
:orphan:
:no-search:
:tocdepth: 2
If this field list appears before other markup, Sphinx stores it as metadata and does not render it as page content.
Source: https://www.sphinx-doc.org/en/master/usage/restructuredtext/field-lists.html

Supported special file-wide metadata fields include:
:tocdepth:
:nocomments:
:orphan:
:no-search: (:nosearch: old alias)
Source: https://www.sphinx-doc.org/en/master/usage/restructuredtext/field-lists.html
For Python API docs, Sphinx supports object metadata via info fields in directives/docstrings:
:param:, :type:, :returns:, :rtype:, :raises:
:meta ...: for hidden metadata (used by autodoc filtering, e.g. :meta private:)
Source: https://www.sphinx-doc.org/en/master/usage/domains/python.html
Templates receive metadata via the meta variable, and rendered HTML meta tags via metatags.
Source: https://www.sphinx-doc.org/en/master/development/html_themes/templating.html
What this means for your envelope system:

Keep your sidecar JSON as source of truth.
Add an exporter that writes Sphinx top-of-file field lists for docs pages.
For Python API docs, map your sidecar fields to :meta: entries in object docs.
Do not rely on decorative banner text for Sphinx indexing; Sphinx keys off structured fields/directives.
