Yes. The key is to keep it deterministic, dependency-free, and “skill-derived” so it is unique without being random.

Design: ICON_STYLE_V1 (Deterministic, Skill-Derived)
Goal: given a skill folder name (and optionally its SKILL.md frontmatter), generate a unique small and large SVG pair.

Inputs
Primary seed: skill_dir.name
Optional extra seed material (if present, still deterministic):
SKILL.md frontmatter name, description
openai.yaml name
Final seed string: f"{skill_dir.name}|{frontmatter_name}|{frontmatter_desc[:64]}"
Deterministic style selection
Compute digest = sha256(seed).digest() and map bytes to:

Palette:
background color: HSL with hue = digest[0] * 360 / 255, sat = 55 + digest[1] % 30, light = 30 + digest[2] % 25
accent color: hue offset by + (digest[3] % 120) + 30
Geometry “motif” (pick one based on digest[4] % N):
diagonal split background
concentric rings
corner badge
stripes (2–5 stripes based on digest[5] % 4)
cutout notch
Glyph:
monogram from the skill name (first letter, or first 2 letters if it reads well)
optional sub-mark (tiny dot/bar) based on digest[6]
Output constraints
Always generate both:
*-small.svg (16x16)
*-large.svg (64x64)
Same motif across sizes, scaled appropriately.
No external dependencies; pure string formatting.
